The baseline commit in this repository,
`baseline: upstream openchatbi v1.0.0b1 (unmodified)`,
is an exact copy of the upstream tree. Everything after it is this fork's work;
`git diff <baseline>..HEAD` is therefore the complete, reviewable delta.

What this fork claims
---------------------
Only the commits authored after the baseline commit. In particular:

* `openchatbi/semantic/` — the semantic layer (metric definitions with
  dependency closure, the table-relationship graph, and column-level business
  rules), which is the single source of truth consumed by both the chat
  text2sql pipeline and the patrol job.
* `openchatbi/patrol/` — a proactive patrol job whose plan is *derived from*
  the semantic layer rather than hand-written SQL.
* The tests, docs and demo dataset shipped alongside those two.

What this fork does not claim
-----------------------------
The upstream product design, the LangGraph agent/text2sql orchestration, the
catalog storage backends, the data analysis agent, the observability stack, and
the evaluation harness — all of that belongs to the upstream author.

Third-party inspiration
-----------------------
The engineering *patterns* in `openchatbi/patrol/` (four-gate alert dedup,
time-bucket-free alert fingerprints, median/MAD baselines with an IQR fallback,
per-metric run ledger, read-only agent tool constraints) were informed by the
independent fork https://github.com/qihuai823/openchatbi-patrol (MIT). Its code
was not copied; this fork's patrol is driven by the semantic layer instead of a
hand-written YAML of SQL statements, which is what makes it a different
increment rather than a downstream copy.
